 PR/issue templates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Five community files that the project's strict process benefits from
but never had:

- CHANGELOG.md: a Keep-a-Changelog-style file with one entry per
  milestone (M0 -> M9.1). The AI-Handoff is a living status board; the
  changelog is the conventional release-to-release artifact. M10
  ('declaration') explicitly wants 'evidence in writing' — a changelog
  is the natural home.
- SECURITY.md: GitHub surfaces this automatically on the repo's Security
  tab. Documents the determinism threat model (seed + content hash +
  command log = match identity; divergence = bug), the mechanical
  guardrails already in place (architecture_law.rs, source bans, the
  pinned toolchain, the 3-OS x 2-profile CI matrix, cargo audit), and
  the explicit non-goals (no network code in the Alpha; the client's
  float use is presentation-only).
- .editorconfig: 4-space indent for Rust/TOML/YAML, 2-space for
  Markdown/JSON/RON. The repo's existing files already follow this
  convention; .editorconfig just makes editors enforce it. cargo fmt
  remains the source of truth for Rust formatting.
- .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md: the green-gate checklist (fmt +
  clippy + tests dev/release, the architecture law, the source bans,
  the golden-hash rule) baked into the PR body so a contributor cannot
  forget it.
- .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md: a template that captures the
  project's unique reproducibility contract — seed, tick budget, slots,
  two final hashes (for desync detection). A deterministic engine's
  bug reports are unusually actionable when seed+tick are present.

New files only; no source, no tests, no hashes.
